\section{Specifying a transformation}{

Rust Geodesy does not use EPSG codes or \code{"from" -> "to"} CRS pairs. Instead
you describe the transformation directly as a pipeline of operator
primitives, separated by \code{|}, each with \code{key=value} arguments:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{gis:in | utm zone=32 | gis:out
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Coordinates are handled in RG's internal convention (radians, geographic
latitude-first), so geographic input/output is wrapped with an adaptor:
\itemize{
\item \verb{gis:in} / \code{gis:out} take degrees in the GIS order \verb{(longitude, latitude)}
— i.e. the natural \verb{(x, y)} order, recommended for most R users.
\item \verb{geo:in} / \code{geo:out} take degrees in the geodetic order \verb{(latitude, longitude)}.
}
}

\section{Operator families (Rust Geodesy 0.15)}{

\itemize{
\item Projections: \code{utm}, \code{tmerc}, \code{btmerc}/\code{butm} (Bowring series),
\code{merc}, \code{webmerc}, \code{lcc}, \code{laea}, \code{omerc}, \code{somerc}.
\item Datum / reference-frame transforms: \code{cart}, \code{helmert}, \code{molodensky},
\code{gridshift}, \code{deformation}.
\item Geodetic computations: \code{geodesic}, \code{curvature}, \code{gravity}, \code{latitude},
\code{deflection}, \code{permtide}.
\item Adaptors and units: \code{adapt}, \code{axisswap}, \code{unitconvert}, \code{dm}, \code{dms},
the \verb{geo:*} / \verb{gis:*} / \verb{neu:*} / \verb{enu:*} I/O macros, and \code{noop}.
}

Call \code{\link[=geod_ops]{geod_ops()}} for the full list and \code{\link[=geod_validate]{geod_validate()}} to check a
